1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Egypt's pyramids.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The greatest of these monuments was the tallest structure on Earth for over 4,000 years.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:15,000
They contained gigantic stone blocks weighing up to 70 tons.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Yet they were made by a society that didn't even possess the wheel.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Egyptian pyramids on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,940
Inside the famous Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, a series of mysterious passageways lead through the mountain of stone.

7
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:52,200
Passing through a magnificent grand gallery, the main passageway finally ends at a granite-lined vault, deep within the massive structure.

8
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:58,540
Called the King's Chamber, many scholars believe that this is where the Great Khufu was finally laid to rest.

9
00:00:58,541 --> 00:01:00,540
But was he?

10
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:08,540
For centuries these extraordinary edifices have inspired an enduring fascination in people around the world.

11
00:01:08,541 --> 00:01:13,540
Generations have wanted to see them, study them, and unlock their age-old secrets,

12
00:01:13,541 --> 00:01:16,540
such as why and exactly how they were built.

13
00:01:16,541 --> 00:01:19,540
And yet despite the best efforts of researchers,

14
00:01:19,541 --> 00:01:24,540
the solutions to many questions surrounding the pyramids remain tantalizingly obscure.

15
00:01:24,541 --> 00:01:28,720
The lack of concrete evidence from ancient Egyptian sites has complicated the problem.

16
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:34,520
Even the application of the most modern technology has failed to unravel all the mysteries.

17
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:38,000
Recently, however, new theories have been proposed,

18
00:01:38,001 --> 00:01:42,620
which call some of the key generally accepted ideas about the pyramids into question,

19
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:47,420
and that may indeed solve some of the riddles concerning these magnificent achievements.

20
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:52,020
One of the first mysteries about the pyramids is their exact number.

21
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:54,700
Nearly 100 have been discovered in Egypt.

22
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:59,000
Surprisingly, however, at least three pyramids that are mentioned in inscriptions

23
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:02,900
remain obscured by time and the relentless desert sands.

24
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:06,820
It may seem surprising that pyramids have not been located yet,

25
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:11,120
but over thousands of years, their superstructures may have been quarried away

26
00:02:11,121 --> 00:02:14,060
and all that's left is underground or may have been built over.

27
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:22,000
Built during a span of almost 1,000 years, from approximately 2630 to 1640 B.C.,

28
00:02:22,001 --> 00:02:26,540
The pyramids stand as cultural and engineering marvels of staggering proportions.

29
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:29,960
Not only are they incredibly magnificent structures,

30
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:34,340
but of course there are also the few wonders of the ancient world to survive.

31
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:39,020
The purpose of the pyramid is to serve as the king's tomb,

32
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,120
but that's only the beginning because we have to understand

33
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:45,400
why the king of Egypt needed to have a tomb quite like that.

34
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:51,760
The pyramid is the architectural realization of the king's ascent to the sun.

35
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,420
That was the king's after-death fate.

36
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:59,680
The pyramid is the spot in which that great transformation takes place in which the king is reborn.

37
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,420
An ancient Arab proverb reads,

38
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:11,120
Man fears time, but time fears the pyramids.

39
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:16,860
And yet the so-called golden age of pyramid building lasted for only 150 years,

40
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:19,900
during the fourth dynasty of the Egyptian Old Kingdom.

41
00:03:19,901 --> 00:03:27,120
And the greatest of the pyramids, those on the Giza Plateau near Cairo, were constructed over a span of only three generations.

42
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:36,940
Remarkable engineering achievements in their own right, the pyramids were also the centerpieces of often vast complexes of temples and smaller tombs called necropolis.

43
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:41,060
The word necropolis literally means city of the dead.

44
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:47,380
So with the pyramid itself, we only have the monument over the king's tomb.

45
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,500
Attached to the pyramid is a temple.

46
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,920
Then there's a long causeway that leads to the valley where there's another temple.

47
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:57,160
Offerings are presented at the statues of the king in this temple.

48
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:01,220
There is enormous staff that's involved for the upkeep of the temple.

49
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:07,180
The legendary Sphinx, also at Giza, is part of one of these necropolies.

50
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:11,280
That built around the pyramid of King Khufu's son, Khafre.

51
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:14,940
We may know the reason why the pyramids were built,

52
00:04:14,941 --> 00:04:18,020
but the origin of their specific shape is still uncertain.

53
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:23,740
The earliest Egyptian burials involved placing the body in a shallow pit,

54
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:26,940
then covering it with a large rock or mound of stones.

55
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:30,260
These not only served to keep jackals from digging up the dead,

56
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:33,880
but also were probably meant to represent the primordial mound

57
00:04:33,881 --> 00:04:36,460
from which the Egyptians believed all life sprang.

58
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:41,840
The ancient Egyptians were oriented towards the Nile,

59
00:04:41,841 --> 00:04:45,020
which was the source of their life and their sustenance.

60
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,960
And the Nile used to flood every year.

61
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,220
After that was over, when the floods would recede,

62
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:53,320
the land would become visible again.

63
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:56,240
So that became a metaphor for creation.

64
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:02,060
So the primeval mound was the first time the first land began to be seen

65
00:05:02,061 --> 00:05:05,700
when the first waters, the primeval waters, had begun to recede.

66
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:09,980
And it was upon this primeval mound that the sun rose for the first time.

67
00:05:09,981 --> 00:05:13,740
Then this mound actually is a pyramid.

68
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:20,180
It represents the primordial mound, that the God created whole Egypt through this mound.

69
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:24,460
The symbol of this primordial mound was the sacred benben,

70
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:30,060
a pyramidal-shaped stone that most Egyptologists believe would later influence the form of the pyramids.

71
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:34,480
And in the top of the pyramid, the capstone is a benben.

72
00:05:34,481 --> 00:05:39,640
Then the capstone that they put it in the top of the pyramid and they cased it with

73
00:05:39,641 --> 00:05:43,660
electrum, white gold and yellow gold.

74
00:05:43,661 --> 00:05:47,940
And that capstone is a symbol that the pyramid is finished.

75
00:05:47,941 --> 00:05:53,060
But some other scholars cite additional influences for the shape of the pyramids.

76
00:05:53,061 --> 00:05:57,720
One Egyptologist has a provocative new theory based on a migration of desert people into

77
00:05:57,721 --> 00:06:03,480
the Nile Valley over 5,000 years ago, at exactly the time when pyramids began to be built.

78
00:06:03,481 --> 00:06:10,480
According to the theory, the newcomers brought knowledge of the desert landscape with them that might have affected the pyramids form.

79
00:06:10,481 --> 00:06:21,480
A very important thing in the western desert is the fact that the only landforms that persist everywhere are conical or pyramidal landforms.

80
00:06:21,481 --> 00:06:28,480
Pointed hills is the main persisting landscape.

81
00:06:28,481 --> 00:06:36,240
These are three conical structures in the middle of the desert, some 300 miles west

82
00:06:36,241 --> 00:06:37,240
of the Nile.

83
00:06:37,241 --> 00:06:42,380
The ancient Egyptians would have seen these things and would have wanted to build their

84
00:06:42,381 --> 00:06:47,600
structures that would remain in the windy environment in that form.

85
00:06:47,601 --> 00:06:53,240
These natural landforms can definitely become, in my mind, the precursors to the pyramids.

86
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:05,380
But before actual pyramids were built, Egyptian tombs went through another stage of development, the mastaba.

87
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:10,920
Mastabas were low trapezoidal structures with sloping sides and flat roofs.

88
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:15,480
Usually made of mud bricks, they developed from the primordial mound burials.

89
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:20,480
Mastaba, and it's the Arabic word for bench, but they're bigger than any bench.

90
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:24,920
But the mastaba is the word for the superstructure of a private tomb.

91
00:07:24,921 --> 00:07:28,000
The Egyptian tomb really consists of two parts.

92
00:07:28,001 --> 00:07:31,300
There's the below ground part, which is the actual burial chamber,

93
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:34,540
and there is the above ground part, which could be cut into the cliff,

94
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:37,980
or it could be a constructed monument like a mastaba.

95
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:42,920
With mastabas, the Egyptians had taken the first step towards the classic pyramid.

96
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:47,780
The next stage came around 2630 BC, during the Egyptian Third Dynasty,

97
00:07:48,260 --> 00:07:49,600
during the reign of King Djoser,

98
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:52,960
when Djoser's high priest and architect, Imhotep,

99
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:55,960
began building the famous step pyramid at Saqqara.

100
00:07:55,961 --> 00:08:03,100
the dynasty three which is before dynasty four there was a genius architect his name is him

101
00:08:03,101 --> 00:08:11,200
hot and he began to make a revolution a building changing the building from mud break to limestone

102
00:08:11,201 --> 00:08:18,880
and he built what's called the step pyramid a mastapa above the one until he reached six

103
00:08:18,881 --> 00:08:25,720
was tapas and that was a revolution soaring to almost 200 feet in height

104
00:08:25,721 --> 00:08:31,900
with a base measuring 397 by 358 feet the step pyramid is thought by many

105
00:08:31,901 --> 00:08:35,860
scholars to be the first large building in human history made completely of

106
00:08:35,861 --> 00:08:41,680
stone and the first pyramid of any kind built in Egypt Imhotep remains one of the

107
00:08:41,681 --> 00:08:46,780
few overseers of a pyramid whose name is still known exactly how he designed and

108
00:08:46,781 --> 00:08:49,620
plan the structure is not.

109
00:08:49,621 --> 00:08:56,060
We have from later times a papyrus where they planned a temple and they knew in advance

110
00:08:56,061 --> 00:09:00,960
how many people they would need, how many food they would have to bring to the place

111
00:09:00,961 --> 00:09:03,000
and how many stones would be needed.

112
00:09:03,001 --> 00:09:08,020
And so there must have been similar papers for all these pyramids, but unfortunately

113
00:09:08,021 --> 00:09:10,220
we don't have them.

114
00:09:10,221 --> 00:09:15,560
However, we do know how the workmen actually accomplished the construction.

115
00:09:15,561 --> 00:09:24,100
To move the stone they used levers, wedges, and rope, usually pulled by teams of men,

116
00:09:24,101 --> 00:09:30,040
chanting, heaving at a one, two, three, heave sort of thing.

117
00:09:30,041 --> 00:09:36,820
To cut the stone they used copper chisels, which are softer than modern iron chisels,

118
00:09:36,821 --> 00:09:39,620
but limestone is a soft stone.

119
00:09:39,621 --> 00:09:43,560
Of course it takes a lot of work, but they had the manpower to do it.

120
00:09:43,561 --> 00:09:49,960
The result is a monument that is as precise as it is imposing.

121
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:53,400
Despite the remarkable symmetry of the final structure, however,

122
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:59,780
archaeological investigations have proven that Imhotep did not plan for the monument to be a step pyramid from the beginning.

123
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:03,620
He started with a mastaba, then he built a larger mastaba,

124
00:10:03,621 --> 00:10:09,140
and then he started piling them one on top of the other so you have as though you have a stairway to heaven.

125
00:10:09,141 --> 00:10:14,140
Why the Steppe Pyramid was enlarged in these stages remains unknown.

126
00:10:14,141 --> 00:10:20,140
As magnificent an edifice as the Steppe Pyramid was, few later pharaohs copied it.

127
00:10:20,141 --> 00:10:26,140
Instead, they soon moved on to a new form, one that provided new challenges to their builders,

128
00:10:26,141 --> 00:10:30,140
and that would change the shape of the pyramids forever.

129
00:10:30,141 --> 00:10:37,140
The designer of the Steppe Pyramid, Imhotep, was so revered that in later centuries he was worshipped as a god.

130
00:10:37,141 --> 00:10:41,140
Egyptian pyramids will return on Modern Marvels.

131
00:10:41,141 --> 00:10:46,140
We now return to Egyptian pyramids on Modern Marvels.

132
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:53,140
Just over a half century after King Djoser built the Step Pyramid in 2630 BC,

133
00:10:53,141 --> 00:10:56,140
King Sneferu came to the throne of Egypt.

134
00:10:56,141 --> 00:11:00,140
He would become the greatest pyramid builder in Egyptian history.

135
00:11:00,141 --> 00:11:05,140
Unsatisfied with his early efforts, Sneferu erected no less than three huge pyramids

136
00:11:05,141 --> 00:11:09,420
pyramids during a reign that may have lasted up to 50 years.

137
00:11:09,421 --> 00:11:13,500
The first was the so-called collapsed pyramid at Maidoum,

138
00:11:13,501 --> 00:11:16,660
begun around 2575 BC.

139
00:11:16,661 --> 00:11:18,760
During its building, Sneferu decided

140
00:11:18,761 --> 00:11:22,040
to change the form of the collapsed pyramid several times.

141
00:11:22,041 --> 00:11:24,480
Why he did so remains unknown.

142
00:11:24,481 --> 00:11:27,900
How he did it is understood much more completely.

143
00:11:27,901 --> 00:11:30,740
It was built as a step pyramid originally,

144
00:11:30,741 --> 00:11:33,640
which was then increased in size.

145
00:11:33,641 --> 00:11:37,480
Then King Sneferu, the inventor of the true pyramid,

146
00:11:37,481 --> 00:11:41,080
decided to change it into four smooth sides

147
00:11:41,081 --> 00:11:43,580
that met at a point high in the sky.

148
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:50,840
And for me, that was the most amazing feat

149
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:54,200
that I've seen in my studies.

150
00:11:55,660 --> 00:11:59,180
Because if you deviate the angle of one side,

151
00:11:59,181 --> 00:12:01,140
even a little bit on the bottom,

152
00:12:01,141 --> 00:12:04,160
it will be exaggerated as you rise to the top.

153
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:05,360
And they never meet.

154
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:07,960
You never get to the Pyramidian, to the apex.

155
00:12:09,740 --> 00:12:12,200
Today, all that is visible of this pyramid

156
00:12:12,201 --> 00:12:14,180
is part of its inner stepped core,

157
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:16,340
surrounded by heaps of loose rubble,

158
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:18,740
thus giving it its collapsed appearance.

159
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:22,260
In earlier times, archaeologists believed

160
00:12:22,261 --> 00:12:24,540
that it had indeed collapsed during construction

161
00:12:24,541 --> 00:12:26,560
and was for this reason abandoned.

162
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:28,700
But later research indicates

163
00:12:28,701 --> 00:12:30,660
that the pyramid was successfully completed,

164
00:12:30,661 --> 00:12:33,120
then was systematically stripped of its casing.

165
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:37,700
Over the ages, that exterior was quarried, used for other buildings.

166
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:42,120
Whole cities could be built from the stone that had been gathered there for the pyramids,

167
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:45,320
and that accounts for its present appearance.

168
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:49,680
Like the step pyramid completed 55 years earlier,

169
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:54,420
the core of the collapsed pyramid was made with layers of stone that actually leaned inward.

170
00:12:54,421 --> 00:13:00,780
The Step Pyramid of Maidum was built with what was called accretion layers.

171
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:06,780
They are layers of stone that are built circumferentially around a core.

172
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:09,720
And they lean in, actually.

173
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:16,480
One thing that is known for certain is that at some point during its construction,

174
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:19,280
the collapsed pyramid was temporarily abandoned.

175
00:13:19,281 --> 00:13:26,020
For reasons that we do not know, in year 17, he left this pyramid completely unfinished.

176
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:31,840
And he built in the ashore the pyramid that we call it now the Bent Pyramid.

177
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:38,880
The Bent Pyramid is really constructed.

178
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:42,800
They started 54 degrees and they could not continue.

179
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:44,220
They had to switch the angle.

180
00:13:44,221 --> 00:13:49,980
The Bent was the first pyramid designed from the beginning to be a true smooth-sided pyramid.

181
00:13:49,981 --> 00:13:58,120
Part of the reason it still looks that way is because, unlike almost all the other pyramids, most of its exterior casing stones remain in place.

182
00:13:58,660 --> 00:14:09,220
With a base measuring 617 feet square and a height of 345 feet, the Bent pyramid is considerably larger than both the collapsed pyramid and the step pyramid.

183
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:13,020
However, soon after construction began, problems appeared.

184
00:14:13,021 --> 00:14:15,520
problems that are still visible in the structure today.

185
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:21,660
They built up ways and discovered that cracks were beginning to form,

186
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:23,840
internally and externally.

187
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:29,360
So about halfway up, they changed the angle of the rise of the sides.

188
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:34,360
The problem was the pyramid was built on a clay subsoil.

189
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:37,160
It couldn't support the weight of the pyramid,

190
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:40,480
and that was the reason for the cracking, but they didn't know it at the time.

191
00:14:40,481 --> 00:14:47,020
The method of laying the masonry on the upper portion of the pyramid was also modified.

192
00:14:47,021 --> 00:14:50,640
Instead of the earlier technique of the stones leaning inward to match the angle of the outer

193
00:14:50,641 --> 00:14:55,980
slope, the later courses were assembled in nearly horizontal layers.

194
00:14:55,981 --> 00:15:00,000
This was not only easier to accomplish for the workers and masons who built the pyramid,

195
00:15:00,001 --> 00:15:03,140
but also made the structure far more stable.

196
00:15:03,141 --> 00:15:07,140
With these engineering lessons learned, Snefru then undertook the building of a brand new

197
00:15:07,141 --> 00:15:11,180
pyramid, one that is still hailed as among the most beautiful and perfect

198
00:15:11,181 --> 00:15:14,140
pyramids ever constructed, the Red Pyramid.

199
00:15:14,141 --> 00:15:20,140
They went just one kilometer to the north and built this massive stone

200
00:15:20,141 --> 00:15:25,140
building behind us which is called the Red Pyramid because of its appearance,

201
00:15:25,141 --> 00:15:34,140
which is better to be seen in the morning light when all these stones really have a red touch.

202
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:43,980
With a base 772 feet square and a height of 345 feet,

203
00:15:44,380 --> 00:15:47,820
the Red Pyramid is more massive but no taller than the Bent Pyramid.

204
00:15:48,180 --> 00:15:50,420
With an angle of approximately 43 degrees,

205
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:54,740
the slope of the Red Pyramid is also much shallower than the lower portion of its bent cousin.

206
00:15:54,741 --> 00:15:59,800
The angle was an attempt to copy the upper angle of the Bent Pyramid

207
00:15:59,801 --> 00:16:05,940
because they felt that that was the safest angle to build a pyramid with.

208
00:16:05,941 --> 00:16:11,720
But structurally, the Red Pyramid's most important feature was the way in which its stones were assembled.

209
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:20,180
The Red Pyramid was an advance over the earlier pyramids because the entire pyramid was built with courses of horizontal stone,

210
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:22,360
a much more stable structure.

211
00:16:22,960 --> 00:16:26,800
All the forces are directly down instead of angled outwardly.

212
00:16:28,260 --> 00:16:34,540
The Red Pyramid of Sneferu is the first pyramid that is actually a perfect pyramid,

213
00:16:34,541 --> 00:16:38,140
that has achieved the pure abstract pyramidal form.

214
00:16:39,860 --> 00:16:43,140
No one knows exactly why Sneferu built this edifice,

215
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:47,420
but archaeologists have speculated that he wanted his final resting place

216
00:16:47,421 --> 00:16:49,700
to be an ideal expression of the pyramid,

217
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:52,100
and the Red Pyramid is certainly that.

218
00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:55,360
With the construction of the Red Pyramid,

219
00:16:55,780 --> 00:16:59,040
all the major engineering and structural elements of the classic pyramid

220
00:16:59,041 --> 00:17:00,340
were put in place.

221
00:17:00,341 --> 00:17:06,340
The stage was set for Sneferu's son Khufu to build the greatest pyramid of them all.

222
00:17:08,340 --> 00:17:16,340
Taken together, the three pyramids built by King Sneferu contain a volume of stone almost a million cubic meters greater than the Great Pyramid.

223
00:17:17,340 --> 00:17:20,340
Egyptian pyramids will return on Modern Marvels.

224
00:17:21,340 --> 00:17:25,340
We now return to Egyptian pyramids on Modern Marvels.

225
00:17:25,341 --> 00:17:34,660
Near the modern city of Cairo lies the largest and most imposing concentration of pyramids in the world, those of the Giza Plateau.

226
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:47,807
They include the biggest of them all, the Great Pyramid of Khufu, begun around 2551 BC, the Pyramid of Khufu's son, Khafre, the Pyramid of Khufu's grandson, Menkare,

227
00:17:47,808 --> 00:17:53,620
several smaller pyramids called Queen's Pyramids, and the legendary Sphinx.

228
00:17:55,340 --> 00:18:00,340
The site of Giza was uniquely suited for the construction of pyramids.

229
00:18:00,341 --> 00:18:04,340
It has the best quality of limestone.

230
00:18:04,341 --> 00:18:07,340
It's about 300 feet above the sea level.

231
00:18:07,341 --> 00:18:09,340
It's a perfect place.

232
00:18:09,341 --> 00:18:11,340
It has the best quality of the stones.

233
00:18:11,341 --> 00:18:15,340
And this type of stones never existed any place else.

234
00:18:19,340 --> 00:18:23,340
Giza was also less than a mile from the banks of the Nile,

235
00:18:23,341 --> 00:18:27,340
making the movement of manpower and supplies to the site far easier.

236
00:18:27,341 --> 00:18:30,340
The Nile was the great artery of ancient Egypt.

237
00:18:30,341 --> 00:18:34,340
There were no roads per se, so the river was instrumental.

238
00:18:34,341 --> 00:18:37,340
Stone was shipped over the river in barges,

239
00:18:37,341 --> 00:18:41,340
not only limestone from one side of the river to the other side of the river,

240
00:18:41,341 --> 00:18:45,340
but granite which came from hundreds of miles away to the south.

241
00:18:45,341 --> 00:18:48,340
Before construction of the Great Pyramid could begin,

242
00:18:48,341 --> 00:18:52,340
its builders had to find a way to make the site completely flat.

243
00:18:52,341 --> 00:18:56,840
Many Egyptologists believe that water was used.

244
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:01,440
First, a shallow channel was dug around the site, then it was filled with water.

245
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:06,420
Since the surface of the water would be exactly the same height in every part of the channel,

246
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:10,720
the Egyptians only had to flatten the dry portions down to the level of the water.

247
00:19:11,440 --> 00:19:15,060
But not everyone agrees that this is how the site for the Great Pyramid was leveled.

248
00:19:16,620 --> 00:19:22,000
Martin Eisler is a sculptor who has studied Egyptian construction techniques for over 30 years.

249
00:19:22,340 --> 00:19:25,640
He has several provocative theories about the building of the pyramids,

250
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:29,660
which are supported by decades of painstaking research and observation.

251
00:19:30,340 --> 00:19:36,840
I do not think that the Great Pyramid was leveled by means of water in any form.

252
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:44,920
I think they did it with no more complicated a device than this instrument called a square level.

253
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:51,680
It's actually an A-frame, as you can see, with two legs and a plumb bob hanging from its apex.

254
00:19:52,340 --> 00:19:59,940
There's a mark in the center that tells where, when the object is level. You level

255
00:19:59,941 --> 00:20:06,420
a small area with this and erect two posts, perhaps seven feet apart. You

256
00:20:06,421 --> 00:20:12,520
mount a board on the post. You could take another post of equal height to the

257
00:20:12,521 --> 00:20:19,660
first two and place it at a distance. Using the board as a sighting rod, you

258
00:20:19,661 --> 00:20:27,100
You could then cut drafts in the bedrock until the pole is at a level with the first two

259
00:20:27,101 --> 00:20:30,460
poles in the small area that you've cut.

260
00:20:30,461 --> 00:20:33,800
Let's call it the leveling area.

261
00:20:33,801 --> 00:20:38,220
This process could then be repeated until the entire base of the pyramid was leveled.

262
00:20:38,221 --> 00:20:43,240
Yet, while experts may disagree on the methods used to level the Great Pyramid, they all

263
00:20:43,241 --> 00:20:46,980
agree that the results were remarkable.

264
00:20:46,981 --> 00:20:50,980
They did an astonishing job on leveling the Great Pyramid.

265
00:20:50,981 --> 00:20:57,980
It deviates by less than an inch from the northern corner to the southern corner,

266
00:20:57,981 --> 00:21:00,980
which is a fantastic accomplishment.

267
00:21:01,980 --> 00:21:04,980
The next stage was laying out the base.

268
00:21:04,981 --> 00:21:09,980
This was important because the orientation of the pyramid was crucial for religious reasons.

269
00:21:09,981 --> 00:21:15,980
You often read about how closely the pyramids are aligned to the cardinal points,

270
00:21:15,981 --> 00:21:17,760
north, south, east, and west.

271
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:22,760
Because just as the pyramid points heavenward towards the sky,

272
00:21:23,080 --> 00:21:25,760
the sky is also anchored in the earth by the pyramids

273
00:21:25,761 --> 00:21:29,120
because the Egyptians associated the cardinal points

274
00:21:29,121 --> 00:21:35,140
with the circuit of the sun, with the sky, and with various constellations.

275
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:40,440
The generally accepted theory is that the pyramids were oriented using stellar observations.

276
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:43,780
By bisecting the angle between the points

277
00:21:43,781 --> 00:21:46,580
where a particular star rises and sets on the horizon.

278
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:49,160
A direction of true north can be obtained,

279
00:21:49,540 --> 00:21:51,340
but other experts, including Martin Eisler,

280
00:21:51,740 --> 00:21:53,740
believe that a different method was used instead.

281
00:21:54,700 --> 00:21:58,700
It's very hard to point to a distant star

282
00:21:58,701 --> 00:22:02,180
and get the accuracy that they were able to achieve.

283
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,160
I think they oriented the pyramids

284
00:22:05,161 --> 00:22:07,920
by means of the shadows cast by the sun.

285
00:22:08,880 --> 00:22:11,900
First, the builders would have placed a perpendicular stake

286
00:22:11,901 --> 00:22:13,600
called a gnomon in the ground.

287
00:22:13,780 --> 00:22:20,980
When equal and opposite angles of the sun cast shadows over a gnomon,

288
00:22:21,660 --> 00:22:26,220
the bisection of that angle gives you a north-south direction.

289
00:22:28,320 --> 00:22:32,180
Whatever the method, the Great Pyramid was aligned almost perfectly,

290
00:22:32,800 --> 00:22:37,740
its sides having a variance of less than 1 15th of one degree from the cardinal points.

291
00:22:38,300 --> 00:22:40,000
Other measurements were equally precise,

292
00:22:40,001 --> 00:22:46,140
each side measuring 756 feet with a discrepancy of only four inches per side.

293
00:22:47,860 --> 00:22:50,540
In order to actually construct this gigantic atavis,

294
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:55,280
the Egyptians faced an equally gigantic problem in human resources management.

295
00:22:55,940 --> 00:23:00,740
Contrary to the popular image, labor was not performed by hundreds of thousands of slaves,

296
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:04,020
but by peasant conscripts as part of a corvée,

297
00:23:04,021 --> 00:23:09,000
that is, as labor owed the state by the peasants, probably as part of their taxes.

298
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:13,200
People are taken from the farms from all over Egypt.

299
00:23:13,580 --> 00:23:16,160
They are brought to a centralized spot, let's say Giza.

300
00:23:16,161 --> 00:23:22,500
They work together in organized gangs directed by experienced masons and architects

301
00:23:22,501 --> 00:23:27,100
to build this great pyramid which becomes a symbol of national identity.

302
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:33,500
The workforce lived and died in its own workers' city, right at the site,

303
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:37,160
which was only discovered and excavated in the early 1990s.

304
00:23:37,161 --> 00:23:45,160
Based on the evidence of our discovery of the size of the workmen camp, we believe only

305
00:23:45,161 --> 00:23:50,100
20,000 workmen were actually involved in the construction of the pyramid.

306
00:23:50,101 --> 00:23:56,620
We believe that there was permanent workmen, and this could be like 6,000.

307
00:23:56,621 --> 00:24:03,960
Those are actually the workmen who make statues, making technical work, draftsmen, craftsmen,

308
00:24:03,961 --> 00:24:05,120
and things like this.

309
00:24:05,121 --> 00:24:08,040
The workmen who move the stones could be 15,000.

310
00:24:08,360 --> 00:24:09,620
And they work in rotation.

311
00:24:10,060 --> 00:24:11,500
They change them every three months.

312
00:24:12,420 --> 00:24:15,940
These workers were further divided into smaller units called crews.

313
00:24:17,020 --> 00:24:19,940
A crew consists of 2,000 workmen.

314
00:24:20,620 --> 00:24:24,120
And they divide the crew, the 2,000, to two gangs.

315
00:24:24,720 --> 00:24:27,400
Each gang consists of 1,000 workmen.

316
00:24:27,780 --> 00:24:31,160
Each gang had a name, like Friends of Khufu.

317
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:33,620
And each gang had an overseer.

318
00:24:33,621 --> 00:24:38,680
They divide each gang to five phial.

319
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:40,740
A phial is a Greek word.

320
00:24:41,220 --> 00:24:42,840
It means tribe, group.

321
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:46,460
Each group consists of 200 workmen.

322
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:50,980
And they had names like great ones, green ones.

323
00:24:51,620 --> 00:24:54,180
And after that, they divide them to small units.

324
00:24:54,840 --> 00:24:56,240
Ten to twelve workmen.

325
00:24:57,080 --> 00:25:00,440
Near the workers' quarters, their graveyard has also been discovered.

326
00:25:00,441 --> 00:25:07,920
One of the overseers' tombs was even made in the form of a small pyramid, complete with an underground passageway and burial chamber.

327
00:25:09,040 --> 00:25:16,260
The overseers lived a relatively long life, but the common laborers who died at the site did so at significantly younger ages.

328
00:25:17,020 --> 00:25:22,200
All the age of the workmen were between 30 to 35 years old.

329
00:25:22,580 --> 00:25:25,880
But the artisans, their ages were between 40 to 45.

330
00:25:26,280 --> 00:25:29,480
And this gives us an idea about the levels of the workers' community.

331
00:25:29,481 --> 00:25:35,180
The artisans were not carrying stones, were not pulling up stones, they were just carving, and it was a light work.

332
00:25:35,500 --> 00:25:40,960
But these people were buried down, they worked in hard work in constructing the pyramids.

333
00:25:41,440 --> 00:25:47,920
The workers were kept supplied by a small army of merchants and tradesmen, who transported their goods to Giza by boat.

334
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:57,020
Also, in order to feed them, farms have to be established, new areas of land have to be cultivated in order to do this.

335
00:25:57,021 --> 00:26:01,980
there has to be a means of taking the products, the food, and getting them to Giza and storing

336
00:26:01,981 --> 00:26:03,620
them there and redistributing them.

337
00:26:04,760 --> 00:26:09,300
But immense projects such as the Great Pyramid had far larger effects than simply building

338
00:26:09,301 --> 00:26:10,620
a monument for a dead king.

339
00:26:11,120 --> 00:26:17,020
In some ways, you can say that the Great Pyramid building projects built the Egyptian state

340
00:26:17,021 --> 00:26:21,020
because an enormous amount of organization was involved.

341
00:26:21,620 --> 00:26:22,860
It was their space program.

342
00:26:22,980 --> 00:26:26,460
It was the Apollo program of the past, where get all of these people to work together so

343
00:26:26,461 --> 00:26:29,077
they can feel great and they can have an accomplishment.

344
00:26:29,078 --> 00:26:31,180
And they did, and it became one country since

345
00:26:31,181 --> 00:26:31,881
then.

346
00:26:31,882 --> 00:26:37,860
It would be this workforce that would complete the largest engineering project yet

347
00:26:37,861 --> 00:26:40,580
undertaken in human history.

348
00:26:40,581 --> 00:26:46,700
The ancient Greek historian Herodotus reported incorrectly that

349
00:26:46,701 --> 00:26:52,080
the Great Pyramid was built by shifts of 100,000 slaves and that King Khufu forced his daughter

350
00:26:52,081 --> 00:26:55,220
into prostitution to help pay for the massive project.

351
00:26:55,221 --> 00:26:58,960
Egyptian pyramids will return on Modern Marvels.

352
00:26:58,961 --> 00:27:03,320
We now return to Egyptian pyramids on Modern Marvels.

353
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:11,140
The workers who quarried the majority of the building stones

354
00:27:11,141 --> 00:27:14,300
for the pyramids at Giza labored at a site that has only

355
00:27:14,301 --> 00:27:16,580
recently been cleared of debris.

356
00:27:16,581 --> 00:27:20,440
Actually, all the stones for building the Great Pyramid

357
00:27:20,441 --> 00:27:25,320
came from a location of a quarry next door to the Great Pyramid,

358
00:27:25,321 --> 00:27:29,280
about 600 feet from the base of the pyramid.

359
00:27:29,281 --> 00:27:32,560
And we discovered this location.

360
00:27:32,561 --> 00:27:34,520
There's a grid pattern in the ground

361
00:27:34,521 --> 00:27:36,520
from which blocks were removed.

362
00:27:36,521 --> 00:27:39,720
There were trenches built in grids.

363
00:27:39,721 --> 00:27:43,680
They dig down the trenches, which are about two feet wide,

364
00:27:43,681 --> 00:27:47,280
and then they split a layer of stone off.

365
00:27:47,281 --> 00:27:49,320
And they did this layer by layer,

366
00:27:49,321 --> 00:27:55,720
so that there's a bluff above the pyramid now that must be 20-25 feet high.

367
00:27:57,380 --> 00:28:01,760
Workers used simple copper tools, like these preserved in the Cairo Museum,

368
00:28:02,100 --> 00:28:03,600
to rough out the limestone blocks.

369
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:07,600
Afterwards, using tools much like these,

370
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:10,500
masons smoothed the sides and edges of the blocks.

371
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:16,240
Then employing wooden sleds and ropes such as this,

372
00:28:16,520 --> 00:28:18,020
the blocks were dragged to the pyramid.

373
00:28:19,320 --> 00:28:24,000
Most scholars believe that the workers piled up the blocks in sequential horizontal layers,

374
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:29,140
with each layer being precisely squared and leveled until the top of the pyramid was reached.

375
00:28:29,760 --> 00:28:31,960
But again, not all the experts agree.

376
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:40,900
Number one, the slightest imperfection on the bottom would assume a cumulative effect as it reached the top.

377
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:49,240
In addition, when I looked at the Great Pyramid, I could see that almost none of the courses were truly level.

378
00:28:49,320 --> 00:28:54,400
And I realized then that this was not the way to go.

379
00:28:54,900 --> 00:28:56,520
There had to be another approach.

380
00:28:57,400 --> 00:29:01,160
And this is what I devoted many years to finding.

381
00:29:02,500 --> 00:29:04,960
Using the collapsed pyramid as a prototype,

382
00:29:05,500 --> 00:29:08,740
Eisler proposes that most pyramids built after the collapsed pyramid

383
00:29:08,741 --> 00:29:11,040
consist of three stages of construction.

384
00:29:12,100 --> 00:29:14,300
First, an inner stepped core,

385
00:29:14,820 --> 00:29:17,080
not unlike the core of the collapsed pyramid, was built.

386
00:29:17,640 --> 00:29:23,820
Then, the base was enlarged, and the large steps were progressively filled in with a second layer of smaller steps,

387
00:29:24,420 --> 00:29:26,580
creating a shape much closer to a true pyramid.

388
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:32,200
Finally, the small stepped exterior was encased in a layer of finely finished limestone,

389
00:29:32,760 --> 00:29:34,600
creating a true smooth-sided pyramid.

390
00:29:36,480 --> 00:29:39,680
Because so much of the original stone remains on the Great Pyramid,

391
00:29:40,360 --> 00:29:44,200
it is impossible to tell if this three-stage process was used in its construction.

392
00:29:44,201 --> 00:29:51,500
But as evidence for his theory, Eisler points to a number of smaller pyramids, called Queen's Pyramids,

393
00:29:51,780 --> 00:29:56,460
that stand alongside the large pyramids at Giza, and which were built at about the same time.

394
00:29:57,840 --> 00:30:02,980
And the remains there show a nucleus of large steps,

395
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:07,740
which were then filled in with what we call packing stones,

396
00:30:07,860 --> 00:30:11,260
which were smaller stones and brought it to a more pyramidal shape.

397
00:30:11,261 --> 00:30:18,940
and finally this whole thing was encased with a mantle of smooth limestone that met at a point in the top.

398
00:30:20,540 --> 00:30:25,220
There are also several theories concerning how the stones were raised to the height of the upper courses.

399
00:30:26,240 --> 00:30:31,180
Most of them involved the construction of ramps that either spiraled around the pyramid as it grew

400
00:30:31,181 --> 00:30:34,160
or led directly to the current level of construction.

401
00:30:35,080 --> 00:30:39,480
The ramp that the Egyptian used to construct the pyramid was huge.

402
00:30:39,481 --> 00:30:42,300
constructed of a stone rubble and mud.

403
00:30:43,080 --> 00:30:45,640
Then when the Egyptians would dismantle this ramp,

404
00:30:46,260 --> 00:30:49,280
they had to leave evidence under the ground of that ramp.

405
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:52,760
I did excavate on the south side,

406
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:58,220
and the surprise was discovering a small section of the ramp.

407
00:30:58,920 --> 00:31:01,500
But some experts, including Martin Eisler,

408
00:31:01,980 --> 00:31:03,880
believe that if a large ramp was employed,

409
00:31:04,600 --> 00:31:06,400
its remains would be far more evident.

410
00:31:06,401 --> 00:31:13,620
You can't erase a ramp that may have reached a height of 500 feet.

411
00:31:13,900 --> 00:31:17,020
The remains would be obvious by aerial photography.

412
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:19,960
Mr. Eisler does think ramps were used,

413
00:31:20,180 --> 00:31:23,300
but only on the lower, more easily accessible levels of a pyramid.

414
00:31:25,080 --> 00:31:28,640
For the higher stages, he believes another method was employed.

415
00:31:29,360 --> 00:31:32,000
I believe they got the stones up the pyramid

416
00:31:32,001 --> 00:31:36,380
by putting a slideway of planks along the side

417
00:31:36,381 --> 00:31:41,420
and pulling the stone by a crew of men who are located up the pyramid

418
00:31:41,421 --> 00:31:47,480
and snubbing the rope around a log which is further up the pyramid.

419
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:56,080
The whole process can be aided considerably by using levers as the stone is elevated

420
00:31:56,081 --> 00:32:01,580
so that with the aid of levers and with the aid of crews of men

421
00:32:01,581 --> 00:32:06,540
standing uphill, I believe they could pull the stones into position.

422
00:32:08,480 --> 00:32:13,240
Indeed, at many pyramids, notches in the building stones which may have been used for the placement

423
00:32:13,241 --> 00:32:14,994
of levers are still visible.

424
00:32:14,995 --> 00:32:19,380
Though controversial, the idea of pulling and levering the stones into

425
00:32:19,381 --> 00:32:23,334
place does address the shortcomings of the various ramp theories.

426
00:32:23,335 --> 00:32:25,220
But as of yet, no one knows for

427
00:32:25,221 --> 00:32:30,060
certain exactly how the ancient Egyptians, a society that didn't even possess the wheel,

428
00:32:30,061 --> 00:32:32,580
accomplished this remarkable feat of engineering.

429
00:32:34,240 --> 00:32:36,020
Once all the blocks were in place,

430
00:32:36,220 --> 00:32:39,260
the Great Pyramid was capped with a triangular piece of granite

431
00:32:39,261 --> 00:32:41,780
meant to represent the sacred Benmen stone,

432
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:43,940
symbol of the primordial mound,

433
00:32:44,480 --> 00:32:46,620
much like this one which was found at Giza,

434
00:32:47,040 --> 00:32:49,900
which may have been coated with a hammered-on layer of gold.

435
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:54,320
Then, using the same methods that were employed to raise and place the other stones,

436
00:32:54,460 --> 00:32:58,640
the body of the pyramid was covered with a casing of fine white limestone.

437
00:32:58,641 --> 00:33:05,640
Shimmering in the desert sun, the final appearance of the pyramid must have been spectacular,

438
00:33:05,641 --> 00:33:09,100
giving those who worked on it an immense sense of pride.

439
00:33:09,101 --> 00:33:14,520
Its final height was 481 feet, making it the tallest structure in the world until the Cologne

440
00:33:14,521 --> 00:33:17,600
Cathedral of Germany topped it in 1880.

441
00:33:17,601 --> 00:33:25,120
The pyramid contains an estimated 2.3 million blocks of stone, each weighing up to 2.5 tons.

442
00:33:25,121 --> 00:33:30,900
Many archaeologists believe that the entire process took almost 23 years.

443
00:33:30,901 --> 00:33:36,440
As impressive as the exterior of the Great Pyramid is, its interior is equally remarkable.

444
00:33:36,441 --> 00:33:41,880
As in the case of all earlier pyramids, there are a series of tunnels and chambers.

445
00:33:41,881 --> 00:33:46,880
The interior arrangements of the pyramid of Khufu are elaborate.

446
00:33:46,881 --> 00:33:52,940
There are three burial chambers and it is not agreed today whether there are supposed

447
00:33:52,941 --> 00:33:54,340
to be three.

448
00:33:54,341 --> 00:33:57,560
was intended from the beginning or whether they were revising their plans.

449
00:33:58,300 --> 00:34:02,560
Because of these multiple chambers and the fact that the pyramid was looted of its contents

450
00:34:02,561 --> 00:34:06,640
long ago, no one knows for certain exactly where Khufu was buried.

451
00:34:07,360 --> 00:34:12,700
But what is believed to be the true burial chamber of the king is reached by a long passageway,

452
00:34:12,701 --> 00:34:19,060
which then turns into an immense ascending gallery, the so-called Grand Gallery, a corbilled

453
00:34:19,061 --> 00:34:23,140
passageway nearly 150 feet long and 26 feet high.

454
00:34:23,141 --> 00:34:28,980
Corbeled means that the headers or short ends of the blocks get successively closer and closer and closer.

455
